/*
 MongoDB — Basic Commands Overview

- This file demonstrates fundamental MongoDB concepts and operations.

1. Database:
   - Databases are containers that hold collections.
   - The "use('dbname')" command switches to an existing database or creates a new one when data is stored.

2. Collections:
   - Equivalent to tables in relational databases.
   - Automatically created when data is first inserted.

3. Documents:
   - Individual records stored inside a collection.
   - Represented in JSON-like (BSON) format.

4. Core Commands Used:
   - insertOne(): Inserts a single document.
   - insertMany(): Inserts multiple documents.
   - find(): Retrieves all matching documents.
   - findOne(): Retrieves only the first matching document.

This file explains how databases, collections, and documents work together,
and how MongoDB dynamically creates collections and databases at runtime.
*/

// MongoDB Basics — Database, Collections, and Documents

// 1. Use or create a database named "college".
// If it doesn’t exist, MongoDB automatically creates it when you first store data.
use('college');

// 2. Create (or switch to) a collection named "student" and insert one document into it.
// A collection in MongoDB is like a "table" in relational databases.
// A document is a single record stored inside a collection (in JSON/BSON format).
// The first time you use insertOne on a new collection, MongoDB automatically creates that collection at runtime.
db.student.insertOne({ name: "Adam", age: 17, marks: 88 });

// 3. Retrieve all documents from the "student" collection.
// This will return every document (record) present inside the collection.
db.student.find();

// 4. Insert multiple documents at once using insertMany().
// If the collection does not exist yet, MongoDB will create it automatically when data is inserted.
db.student.insertMany([
  { name: "bob", marks: 65 },
  { name: "eve", city: "Delhi" }
]);

// 5. Find all documents that have a specific field value.
// This query returns all documents where the field "city" equals "Delhi".
// The result is always returned as an array of matching documents.
db.student.find({ city: "Delhi" });

// 6. Find only one document that matches the condition.
// This command returns a single document (the first match) instead of an array.
db.student.findOne({ city: "Delhi" });
